    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2). Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Changes\n\n**`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` (both optional strings, `maxLength: 255`) to the `OwnerFields` schema. Relaxed the flat `address` field: removed its `@NotBlank` extra-annotation and `minLength`, and removed `address` from the `required` list so structured-only owners are accepted. The contract stays backward-compatible (`firstName`, `lastName`, `city`, `telephone` remain required).\n\n**`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` fields (mapped to new `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns) with getters/setters. The composed value continues to be stored in the existing `address` column, so everything reading `getAddress()` (and the shared `city`/`postcode` used by the household hash, postcode validation and locality) sees the effective address.\n\n**`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Replaced the single `AddressNormalizer.normalize(...)` call in `addOwner` with a `normalizeAddress(owner)` helper that:\n- Prefers the structured form when `addressLine1` is non-blank: normalizes line 1 (and line 2 when present), then composes `address` = normalized line1 `+ \" \" +` normalized line2 (line1 alone when line2 absent).\n- Otherwise normalizes the flat `address` as before.\n- Either way, a blank address is rejected with `400` via `AddressNormalizer.normalize`, enforcing \"valid with an address in either form plus city.\"\n\n**Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 Added `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns to the `owners` table. Tests run against the hsqldb schema.\n\nMapStruct maps the new fields automatically by name, so both request binding (`OwnerFieldsDto \u2192 Owner`) and the response (`Owner \u2192 OwnerDto`) carry `addressLine1`/`addressLine2`, and the response's `address` is the composed normalized string \u2014 matching the acceptance test's expectations (`addressLine1` = `\"10 KING STREET\"`, `address` = `\"10 KING STREET\"`).\n\nThe two acceptance tests (structured-address normalization/composition, and the backward-compatible flat-address path) both pass.",
