    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change reads as a natural, additive extension of the existing conventions in this codebase.\n\n- **Right unit for the logic.** The `addOwner` handler already plays the role of orchestrating the field normalizers (`TelephoneNormalizer`, `EmailNormalizer`, `AddressNormalizer`, the postcode/registration validators). Extracting the address handling into a private `normalizeAddress(Owner)` helper fits that role. A pure stateless utility method (the `AddressNormalizer.normalize(String) -> String` shape) wouldn't fit cleanly here because this step produces *three* coordinated outputs (`addressLine1`, `addressLine2`, and the composed flat `address`); a small private coordinator that reuses `AddressNormalizer.normalize` for the actual string transformation is the idiomatic way to express that, and it keeps the field-shaping rule itself in `AddressNormalizer` where it belongs.\n\n- **No duplication.** `AddressNormalizer` is still the single home for the actual normalization; the helper only decides which fields to feed it and how to compose the result. The V2 owner controller does no owner-address normalization, so there's no parallel path left to drift.\n\n- **Not a catch-all.** The method is single-purpose, cohesive, and clearly documented; it doesn't accrete unrelated responsibilities onto the controller.\n\n- **Model / schema / OpenAPI additions are conventional.** The new `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` fields follow the existing `@Column` + getter/setter style in `Owner`, the four schema dialects are updated consistently, and relaxing `address` from required to optional in `openapi.yml` (keeping `city`) matches the \"valid in either form\" contract described in the spec. Downstream readers (household id, postcode validation, locality/customer-code) continue to work off the composed flat `address`, so nothing had to be threaded through in multiple places.\n\nA maintainer of this repo would find this structure natural.",
